  "text": "To solve this problem, we will work backwards from the given value of $a_n = \\frac{17}{43}$ to find the corresponding $n$.\n\n1. Start with the given value:\n   $$ a_n = \\frac{17}{43} $$\n\n2. We know that when $n$ is a multiple of 3, $a_n = 2 + a_{n/3}$. Since 43 is not a multiple of any power of 2, we use this:\n   $$ a_{n/3} = \\frac{43 - 2}{43} = \\frac{41}{43} $$\n\n3. To get $a_{n/3}$ to $\\frac{17}{43}$, we need to scale it down:\n   $$ a_{n/3} = \\frac{41}{43} = \\frac{326}{43 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{159}{43} $$\n\n4. Now, $a_{n/3} = \\frac{159}{43}$ suggests another similar calculation:\n   $$ a_m = 2 + \\frac{159}{43} = \\frac{134 + 159}{43} = \\frac{293}{43} $$\n\n5. Next, for 293 to be a decrease, we need to consider if it is a multiple of 3 or some other form. We test if it's a multiple of 3:\n   $$ 293 \\mod 3 = 1 $$\n\n6. Since 293 is not a multiple of 3, we apply the opposite formula:\n   $$ a_{293} = \\frac{1}{a_{292}} $$\n\n7. When $n$ is not a multiple of 3, we apply the inverse of the formula:\n   $$ a_{292} = \\frac{134 + 159}{43} = \\frac{452}{43} $$\n\n8. Now, we must scale 452 down to 2:\n   $$ a_{292} = \\frac{452}{43} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{452}{43 \\cdot 9} = \\frac{452}{387} = \\frac{53}{43} $$\n\n9. Working this back up:\n   $$ a_{249} = \\frac{1}{a_{248}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{23}{43}} = \\frac{23}{43} $$\n   $$ a_{234} = 2 + \\frac{23}{43} = \\frac{98}{43} $$\n   $$ a_{231} = \\frac{1}{a_{230}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{231}{43}} = \\frac{43}{231} $$\n   $$ a_{216} = 2 + \\frac{43}{231} = \\frac{467}{231} $$\n   $$ a_{213} = \\frac{1}{a_{212}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{213}{231}} = \\frac{231}{213} = \\frac{77}{71} $$\n\n10. Finally:\n    $$ a_{207} = 2 + \\frac{77}{71} = \\frac{169}{71} $$\n    $$ a_{204} = \\frac{1}{a_{203}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{207}{71}} = \\frac{71}{207} = \\frac{9}{29} $$\n    $$ a_{192} = 2 + \\frac{9}{29} = \\frac{67}{29} $$\n    $$ a_{189} = \\frac{1}{a_{188}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{189}{29}} = \\frac{29}{189} = \\frac{1}{5} $$\n\n11. Finally, checking $n = 81$:\n    $$ a_{80} = 2 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{11}{5} $$\n    $$ a_{81} = \\frac{1}{a_{80}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{11}{5}} = \\frac{5}{11} = \\frac{5}{43} $$\n\nGiven the final form matches condition:\n$$ a_n = \\frac{5}{43} = \\frac{5}{43} $$\nThe final $n$ is $81$.\nAnswer:\n$$",
